<body>
<div class="wrap">

<header class="doc">
  <p class="doc-eyebrow">Electrodeposition electrolyte · Pourbaix analysis</p>
  <h1 class="doc-title">Coupled <span class="cu">Cu</span> · <span class="fe">Fe</span> stability map</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">
    <strong>1&nbsp;mM Cu(II) &amp; 1&nbsp;mM Fe(III)</strong> in a
    <strong>10&nbsp;mM citrate / 10&nbsp;mM glycine / 0.1&nbsp;M NH<sub>3</sub> / 0.1&nbsp;M Cl<sup>−</sup></strong>
    electrolyte, at 25&nbsp;°C and ionic strength <em>I</em>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.1&nbsp;m. Domain: pH 0 – 14, <em>E</em> = −1.0 to +1.5 V vs SHE.
  </p>
  <div class="meta">
    <span>hydrated solids only</span>
    <span>Cu(s), Cu<sub>2</sub>O, Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub></span>
    <span>Fe(s), Fe(OH)<sub>2</sub>, Fe(OH)<sub>3</sub>(am)</span>
    <span>CuO, Fe<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>, FeOOH, Fe<sub>3</sub>O<sub>4</sub> excluded</span>
  </div>
</header>

<section id="approach">
  <h2><span class="num">01</span>Approach</h2>
  <p class="lead">
    The solver treats every complex as forming from the aquo ion plus its listed ligand set with a
    single stability constant β. Ligand acidity ladders set α<sub>Cit<sup>3−</sup></sub>(pH),
    α<sub>Gly<sup>−</sup></sub>(pH) and α<sub>NH<sub>3</sub></sub>(pH). Because
    C<sub>T,Cu</sub> + C<sub>T,Fe</sub> = 2&nbsp;mM sits far below the ligand totals
    (10 – 100&nbsp;mM), free ligand activities are taken as
    C<sub>T,L</sub> · α<sub>L,deprot</sub>(pH), without depletion by metal binding — worst-case
    error &lt;&nbsp;15%.
  </p>
  <p>
    At each pH the solver computes (i) the free-ion mole fractions α<sub>M<sup>n+</sup></sub>
