<section id="implications">
  <h2><span class="num">08</span>Implications for co-electrodeposition</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    <p class="h">The Fe(III)/Fe(II) potential falls below Cu deposition across almost the entire mid-pH range.</p>
    <p>At pH 0 in the aquo picture, Fe(III)/Fe(II) sits at +0.73&nbsp;V and Cu(II)/Cu(0) at
    +0.34&nbsp;V — Fe(III) is a strong oxidiser of Cu(0). In the citrate/glycine/NH<sub>3</sub>
    electrolyte, by pH ≈ 4 the Fe(III)/Fe(II) line is already at +0.24&nbsp;V and Cu(II)/Cu(0)
    is at +0.19&nbsp;V; by pH 8 the Fe(III)/Fe(II) potential is negative and the aqueous
    Cu(II)/Cu(0) transition is near 0&nbsp;V. Citrate has effectively inverted the
    Cu-vs-Fe corrosion picture.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout">
    <p class="h">There is no (pH, E) window where dissolved Cu(II) and dissolved Fe(0) or Fe(II)-only can coexist without Fe(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s).</p>
    <p>Fe(OH)<sub>3</sub>(s, am) starts to form at pH ≈ 7.5, so any Cu-deposition bath
    with pH above ~7 already carries an Fe(OH)<sub>3</sub> phase unless Fe(III) is
    continuously reduced. For a clean Cu bath, either (i) work at pH &lt; 7 where Fe
    is speciated as Fe(Cit)<sub>2</sub><sup>3−</sup>/Fe(II)-citrate, or (ii) work below
    the Fe(III)/Fe(II) boundary so all Fe is Fe(II)-citrate and Fe(OH)<sub>3</sub>
    cannot form.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout">
    <p class="h">Co-deposition of Cu and Fe requires reaching the Fe(II)/Fe(0) line at ≈ −0.55 to −0.85 V.</p>
    <p>The Fe(II)/Fe(0) line is pinned near −0.55&nbsp;V at low pH and drops with
    increasing pH (to about −0.83&nbsp;V under Fe(OH)<sub>2</sub>(s) above pH 9.4).
    Cu deposition, meanwhile, becomes possible at much less negative <em>E</em> — as
    high as +0.13&nbsp;V for Cu(I)&nbsp;→&nbsp;Cu(s) at pH&nbsp;0, or ≈&nbsp;−0.2&nbsp;V for
    Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub>/Cu(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>&nbsp;→&nbsp;Cu(s) at pH 11.
    The ~0.4 – 0.9 V gap between Cu-deposition and Fe-deposition potentials is
    almost entirely due to citrate-glycine-ammonia complexation of Cu(II) — a bare-aquo
    diagram would place Cu deposition 0.34 V positive of Fe and the two would never
    co-deposit at reasonable current densities.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout">
    <p class="h">Cu(I) is a real speciated intermediate here, not a bookkeeping artefact.</p>
    <p>In pure water Cu(I) disproportionates across almost the whole pH range. In this
    electrolyte, chloride opens a CuCl<sub>2</sub><sup>−</sup>/CuCl<sub>3</sub><sup>2−</sup>
    window at pH 0 – 4, and ammonia opens a Cu(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>
    window at pH 7 – 11.5. Any Cu deposition mechanism going through a one-electron
    intermediate is chemically supported in these bands.</p>
  </div>
</section>

